  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nToday: Periods of Showers and Storms\n\nOngoing scattered showers across portions of southeast Minnesota and \nwestern Wisconsin associated with a band of 850-700hPa theta-e \nadvection come to an end overnight as said band continues moving \nnorthward. Further south, nebulous 900-800hPa warm air advection \nspreads through northeast Iowa and southwest Wisconsin during the \nearly morning hours with some scattered showers already ongoing. \nThese showers have greater potential to continue through the early \nmorning hours (20-40%), but little to no impacts expected.\n\nHeading into this afternoon, a 500hPa shortwave trough lifts \nnortheastward out of the Central Plains, forcing a cold front \nthrough the Upper Mississippi River Valley. Instability builds \nacross the region along and ahead of the front with MLCAPE reaching \n750-1250 J/Kg, greatest south of I-90 and west of the Mississippi \nRiver, supporting convective development across the region as the \nboundary moves through (30-50%). CAM guidance has consistently been \nsuggesting storm coverage along the front should be more isolated to \nscattered in nature, likely due to some weak capping across much of \nthe Upper Mississippi River Valley as seen in model soundings. \nHowever, given the combination of the wave aloft, forcing along the \nfront, and evident instability, thinking the environment should be \nable to overcome the weak capping resulting in some greater \nshower/storm coverage this afternoon.  \n\nWhile strong/severe potential is very low (<5%) with any convective \ndevelopment due to 0-6km bulk shear of 15kts or less, some fairly \nefficient rain rates are possible given the juiced-up atmosphere \nthat remains in place across the Midwest. As has been the case over \nthe past several days, PWATs near 2in, warm cloud depths of 10kft+, \nand skinny SBCAPE profiles will support some instances of rainfall \nrates upwards of 1 in/hr. Since we've largely missed out on the \nheavy rainfall over the past few days, flooding concern is very low \nas the front moves through. That said, some local areas of ponding \nseem reasonable where the heaviest rain falls. \n\nSunday - Monday: Drier, Near Normal Temperatures\n\nWith the cold frontal passage tonight, a much drier airmass is \nushered into the Midwest bringing relief from the persistent \npattern of precipitation. High pressure will be the dominant \nfeature across the region resulting in partly cloudy skies to \nend the weekend and start the new week. Some breezier northwest \nwinds look to be on the table Sunday afternoon as cold advection\nsteepens 0-1km lapse rates with 20-25kts of wind atop this \nlayer. That said, not expecting gusts to be overly impressive, \ngenerally only 15-25mph. Stronger winds shift east as we head \ninto Sunday night while high pressure and low level ridging take\nhold. Clearing skies and light winds are currently expected \nacross the region during this period which could result in a \nnight of valley fog, though low level saturation may be a \nlimiting factor given the drier airmass. Monday looks to be a \nrepeat of Sunday albeit with lighter winds. High temperatures \ntop out in the upper 70s to low 80s both days, around normal for\nthis time of year. \n\nTuesday - Wednesday: Increasing Shower and Storm Potential\n\nA more zonal upper level pattern will be in place across the United \nStates Tuesday into Wednesday. A jet streak and associated 500hPa \nshortwave trough over the west coast early Tuesday will be able to \nquickly translate eastward through the day along this zonal flow and \nforce a cold front through the Upper Mississippi River Valley. \nShower and storm potential increase along this front, sitting around \n50-80% in the 15.01z NBM. There does appear to be some potential for \nsome stronger storms during this period as 70-90kts winds associated \nwith the aforementioned jet streak increases 0-6km bulk shear to \naround 40-50kts along with MUCAPE 1000-2000 J/kg present across the \nregion. AI/ML guidance also continue to suggest there is some \nprobability for strong/severe weather during this timeframe so it'll \nremain a period to monitor.",
